 ## Failures
 
-A send that fails raises `RuntimeError` once the client has stopped retrying.
-Treat the alert as unsent and page the secondary by hand.
+A send that exhausts its retries raises `RuntimeError`. A response the client
+will not retry — any 4xx, including 429 — raises `requests.HTTPError` straight
+away, so expect either.
+
+Neither exception means the message was not sent. The client retries whenever it
+cannot confirm delivery, and the usual cause is Kestrel queueing the SMS and
+answering later than the five-second timeout. Check whether the page arrived
+before paging the secondary by hand.
+
+## Duplicate alerts
+
+Two or three copies of one alert seconds apart are one incident, not two or
+three. A single `send` makes up to three attempts and gives up after about
+twenty seconds, so copies landing inside that window came from one call — read
+them as one page and ignore the repeats.
+
+Every attempt for a message carries the same `Idempotency-Key`, so Kestrel can
+recognize a retry as the message it already holds rather than a fresh alert.
+Copies spaced further apart than that twenty-second window are not retries;
+something upstream called `send` more than once, and that is worth chasing.
 
 ## Rate limits
 
